"workspaceRoot":"/Users/latekaapi/Projects/harness","providerId":"meta","modelId":"muse-spark-1.3-contributor","turnCount":2,"forkedFrom":null,"name":"ember-lyra","title":"Research task, no code changes except writing one new file. Workspace: /Users/latekaapi/Projects/harness. Do not commit. Write docs/11-muse-workflows.md: a precise, evidence-based guide to Muse Code 1.1.1's own workflow and subagent facilities, for an operator who drives `muse exec` headless and wants one exec run to fan work out to several subagents or a workflow. Cover, with evidence for each claim (name the tool, skill body, bundled doc, help text, feature flag or config file you read; quote short snippets): 1. What a \"workflow\" is in Muse (the workflow tool / workflow items with children, feature flags `workflow_tool`, `workflow_api_v2_rollout` in ~/.local/share/muse/feature-config). How a workflow is defined (script? JSON? natural language proposal by the model?), where definitions live (workspace files, ~/.config/muse, plugins), what its script or spec API looks like, how children map to subagents, retries/attempts, resume-from-run, and how it is triggered from a prompt in `muse exec`. 2. Subagents: the agent-definition format (`--agents <JSON>` overlay, `muse init` scaffold, workspace agent config), roles, depth, worktree isolation (`--subagent-worktree-isolation`, `-w`), how results come back (SubagentResult: summary, text, artifactRefs), the `read_skill`/tools a subagent gets, and how to spawn N in parallel from one prompt. 3. Cross-session messaging (`muse session-message`) and whether it is useful for orchestration. 4. Limits: max subagents, depth, step caps, what `--max-model-steps` counts, how usage of children is reported, what is billed. 5. A worked recommendation: the exact `muse exec` invocation and prompt shape that makes Muse run a workflow of 4 independent implementation tasks in isolated worktrees, with a final integration/verification step, and what the operator sees in the `--json` stream (event types for workflow/subagent progress) to monitor it. 6. What you could NOT determine, stated plainly. Read your own bundled skills and any bundled docs you can reach (use read_skill on anything relevant, e.g. doctor, read-session, create-skill; look for a workflow or subagent skill), inspect `muse --help`, `muse exec --help`, `muse init --dry-run`, `~/.local/share/muse/feature-config`, `~/.config/muse/settings.json` (never print secrets from auth.json), and the wire schema at fixtures/msp/msp-ts/msp.d.ts (search `workflow`, `subagent`, `WorkflowChild`). Do not spawn subagents or workflows yourself in this run; this is reading only. Keep the doc under 250 lines.","firstUserPrompt":"Research task, no code changes except writing one new file. Workspace: /Users/latekaapi/Projects/harness. Do not commit. Write docs/11-muse-workflows.md: a precise, evidence-based guide to Muse Code 1.1.1's own workflow and subagent facilities, for an operator who drives `muse exec` headless and wants one exec run to fan work out to several subagents or a workflow. Cover, with evidence for each claim (name the tool, skill body, bundled doc, help text, feature flag or config file you read; quote short snippets): 1. What a \"workflow\" is in Muse (the workflow tool / workflow items with children, feature flags `workflow_tool`, `workflow_api_v2_rollout` in ~/.local/share/muse/feature-config). How a workflow is defined (script? JSON? natural language proposal by the model?), where definitions live (workspace files, ~/.config/muse, plugins), what its script or spec API looks like, how children map to subagents, retries/attempts, resume-from-run, and how it is triggered from a prompt in `muse exec`. 2. Subagents: the agent-definition format (`--agents <JSON>` overlay, `muse init` scaffold, workspace agent config), roles, depth, worktree isolation (`--subagent-worktree-isolation`, `-w`), how results come back (SubagentResult: summary, text, artifactRefs), the `read_skill`/